  "live_records": {
    "q-79867": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79867",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Solve the equation ${log}_{3}⁡(x+4)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we use the power rule to simplify the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡({25}^{1/2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\ x+4 & =5 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(1+4) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(25).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
