    "q-27725": {
      "dom_id": "step-q27725",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Solve $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9)$ for $x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9) \\\\ 2x-1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 4x & =-10 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-\\frac{5}{2})-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(6(-\\frac{5}{2})+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-6) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-27725-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "No real solutions",
              "html": "No real solutions"
            },
            {
              "option": "b",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-27725-b",
