    "q-79872": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79872",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4).$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-3)^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-(-3)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡((2)^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3,2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
