    "q-1131": {
      "dom_id": "step-q1131",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Solve the equation ${log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35)={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1).$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & ={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {v}^{2}+35 & =-12v-1 \\\\ {v}^{2}+12v+36 & =0 \\\\ (v+6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡((-6)^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12(-6)-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡(71) & ={log}_{12}⁡(71).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-6$ is a valid solution.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
